From d44e94de2a01c4cd84f31103acee236c1aff20ae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Abdelhafid DJIDEL <266947999+hafid-logic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 21:35:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tests(process): pin ChildStatus word encoding for exit and
 signal

### What
Pins `ChildStatus::word`: an exited child encodes `(code & 0xff) << 8`; a signaled child encodes `signal & 0x7f`.

### Why it matters
The wait status word must match the kernel's encoding. The existing tests do not exercise `word` directly, so a shift-direction or mask fault survives the suite.

### Mutation evidence (fail-then-pass)
| Mutant | Existing suite | New test |
|---|---|---|
| exit `<< 8` -> `>> 8` | PASS (survives) | FAIL (killed) |
| signal `& 0x7f` -> `\| 0x7f` | PASS (survives) | FAIL (killed) |

Baseline (clean tree + new test): PASS. Reproduce: `cargo test -p ginux-process --lib child_status`.

Prepared with AI assistance; reviewed by me.
---
 crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs b/crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs
index b1a7312..15be62c 100644
--- a/crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/ginux-process/src/lib.rs
@@ -2119,4 +2119,13 @@ mod tests {
         assert_eq!(pipes.pipe_ends(id), 0);
         assert_eq!(pipes.active_pipes(), 0);
     }
+
+    #[test]
+    fn child_status_word_encodes_exit_and_signal() {
+        assert_eq!(ChildStatus::Exited(0).word(), 0);
+        assert_eq!(ChildStatus::Exited(1).word(), 1 << 8);
+        assert_eq!(ChildStatus::Exited(255).word(), 255 << 8);
+        assert_eq!(ChildStatus::Signaled(9).word(), 9);
+        assert_eq!(ChildStatus::Signaled(127).word(), 127);
+    }
 }
